\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n![](images/ac/ac086686e36fdc3e3fffbf4e772f5bdd3d797a71cd06a8e0c4c0bc338051371e.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{42}=-2$ and $u_{24}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{42} \\cdot u_{24} = (-2) \\cdot (-3) =6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119609 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119609 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/092a8a3d8ca1efa88189085838d4782072a254ac3790cf01f25ad9b5d0bca0ca.png)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry $l_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ec2663e-be00-560b-8d42-d0e1b1d023c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119609 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to make $0$ at the position $a_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the ${\\color{blue}3}$rd row of $A,$ and we add the ${\\color{red}1}$st row of $A$ multiplied by ${\\color{purple}-4}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry $l_{{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}1}}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to $R_1,$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\nl_{31}=-({\\color{purple}-4})=4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119704 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119704 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8b39a1d4b72f3ab3bfc6758a73e5c1729d9261be539777b45959c342d4bda4da.png)\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119704 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "763fad5f-7cb7-5ff3-a14e-e2849e90906a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119704 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 8 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 6 & -8 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 22 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 11 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u_{33}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119705 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119705 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e819307ecef66a628ff3b0b03d98c6e0f054bbb6c78cd9e7395b99e45f54000a.png)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of $l_{31} \\cdot u_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b19414c3-443f-5075-a2db-f27eb3a7e936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119705 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -12 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 24 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 24 & -9 \\\\ -2 & \\,1\\, & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/1a/1ae6992336eda022f30a643ff0e89ae46c37978ac193370e518ecbea5084878b.png)\n\nFinally, since $l_{31}=-2$ and $u_{23}=-9,$ we get\n\n$$\nl_{31} \\cdot u_{23} = (-2) \\cdot (-9) =18.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186302371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the LU Factorization of a 4x4 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119797 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a86c24c2-97c2-534e-9c03-9735b031bb23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119798 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921861202